--TEST--
Core\Password's two readers answer the same question the same way twice, while two hash calls over one password differ by design and the readers agree about both of them — spec § 16, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The *agreement* shape, over the rule spec § 16's three rows share: `verify`
// and `needsRehash` are functions of their arguments and nothing else, so
// asking either one twice with nothing in between has to answer the same
// thing, while `hash` is the member for which that is the *defect* — its salt
// is fresh per call, so two hashes of one password that matched would be two
// users indistinguishable in the store only until someone looked.
//
// What that rules out is a reader that quietly carried state: a parameter set
// read once and cached, a stored string consumed on the way through, a verdict
// memoised against the wrong key. None of it is visible to a case that asks
// once, which is every other case in this class.
//
// The first answer is pinned as well as compared, because two identical wrong
// answers agree exactly as well as two right ones — a `verify` that always
// answered `false` would be perfectly self-consistent. So each question is
// pinned on its own line and the agreements are counted at the end, and both
// readers are asked one question they answer `true` and one they answer
// `false`.
secret string $password = "correct horse battery staple";
secret string $wrong = "hunter2";
string $stored = Core\Password::hash($password);

// Weaker on memory than this class writes, so `needsRehash` says `true` here.
// Its shape is `password-needs-rehash-answers-weaker-not-different.nvst`'s,
// which is where the reading of it is pinned.
string $behind = '$argon2id$v=19$m=8192,t=2,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';

int $agreed = 0;

bool $accepts = Core\Password::verify($password, $stored);
bool $acceptsAgain = Core\Password::verify($password, $stored);
if ($accepts == $acceptsAgain) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($accepts) {
    echo "verify accepts the right password\n";
} else {
    echo "verify refused the right password\n";
}

bool $refuses = Core\Password::verify($wrong, $stored);
bool $refusesAgain = Core\Password::verify($wrong, $stored);
if ($refuses == $refusesAgain) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($refuses) {
    echo "verify accepted the wrong password\n";
} else {
    echo "verify refuses the wrong password\n";
}

bool $current = Core\Password::needsRehash($stored);
bool $currentAgain = Core\Password::needsRehash($stored);
if ($current == $currentAgain) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($current) {
    echo "a fresh hash reported stale\n";
} else {
    echo "needsRehash leaves a fresh hash alone\n";
}

bool $stale = Core\Password::needsRehash($behind);
bool $staleAgain = Core\Password::needsRehash($behind);
if ($stale == $staleAgain) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($stale) {
    echo "needsRehash still says a thin hash is stale\n";
} else {
    echo "a thin hash reported current\n";
}

// `hash` is the disagreement, and it is one member wide: the bytes differ,
// and both readers say the same thing about either of them. A salt that had
// stopped being fresh shows up on the first line here; a reader that had
// become sensitive to which of two equivalent hashes it was handed shows up on
// the count.
string $again = Core\Password::hash($password);
if ($again == $stored) {
    echo "two hashes of one password matched\n";
} else {
    echo "two hashes of one password differ\n";
}
if (Core\Password::verify($password, $again) == $accepts) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if (Core\Password::needsRehash($again) == $current) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

echo "agreed=", $agreed, " of 6\n";
--EXPECT--
verify accepts the right password
verify refuses the wrong password
needsRehash leaves a fresh hash alone
needsRehash still says a thin hash is stale
two hashes of one password differ
agreed=6 of 6
